'''State Route&nbsp;157''' ('''SR&nbsp;157''') is a {{convert|91|mi|km|adj=mid|-long}} [[State highway (US)|state highway]] in the [[North Alabama|northern]] and [[Northwest Alabama|northwestern]] parts of the U.S. state of [[Alabama]]. Its southern terminus is at an intersection with [[U.S. Route 278 in Alabama|U.S. Route&nbsp;278]] (US&nbsp;278) approximately {{convert|10|mi|km}} east of [[Cullman, Alabama|Cullman]], where it continues as County Road 719. The northern terminus of the highway is at the [[Tennessee]] state line northwest of [[Florence, Alabama|Florence]], where it continues as [[Tennessee State Route&nbsp;227]] (SR&nbsp;227).

==Route description==
SR&nbsp;157 begins at a 4-way stop with US 278. South of the 4-way stop, the road continues as Cullman County Road 719.

Throughout North Cullman, SR&nbsp;157 is oriented in an east-to-west trajectory, passing by a large [[Buick]]/[[General Motors Company|GMC]] dealership at its junction with [[U.S. Route 31|U.S. 31]], then past several restaurants, and ultimately reaching [[Interstate 65|I-65]]. In northern Cullman County, the route makes a gradual curve north. The county line with [[Morgan County, Alabama|Morgan County]] is marked in the middle of a steep downgrade/upgrade known as Battleground Mountain for the nearby town of [[Battleground, Alabama|Battleground]].

After this descent/ascent, the route winds its way to Danville, where it junctions with CR-55 and CR-41 (Danville Road). It crosses the line into [[Lawrence County, Alabama|Lawrence County]] and shortly junctions with [[Alabama State Route 36|SR&nbsp;36]], which leads to [[Hartselle, Alabama|Hartselle]] and [[Wren, Alabama|Wren]].

Eventually, the route reaches [[Moulton, Alabama|Moulton]], where it serves as the main north-to-south thoroughfare for the east district of Moulton, which contains most of its restaurants and shops. It eventually junctions with [[Alabama State Route 24|SR&nbsp;24]] ([[Corridor V (Appalachian Development Highway System)|Corridor&nbsp;V]]) and [[Alabama State Route 33|SR&nbsp;33]].

Past this point, the route passes through [[Hatton, Lawrence County, Alabama|Hatton]] and [[Alabama State Route 101|SR&nbsp;101]]. It eventually reaches the [[Colbert County, Alabama|Colbert County]] line and thence junctions with [[Alabama State Route 20|SR&nbsp;20]].

The concurrent routes reach [[Muscle Shoals, Alabama|Muscle Shoals]], where the route turns north in a concurrency with [[Alabama State Route 133|SR&nbsp;133]], losing SR&nbsp;20.

Past this point, the route crosses the Tennessee River and the [[Lauderdale County, Alabama|Lauderdale County]] line. The route passes through [[Florence, Alabama|Florence]] on a freeway directly through town, intersecting with [[U.S. Route 72|U.S. 72]] and [[U.S. Route 43|U.S. 43]], also losing SR&nbsp;133 and gaining [[Alabama State Route 17|SR&nbsp;17]] before and at this specific interchange respectively. On the north side of town, the route re-engages in a concurrency with SR&nbsp;133 and now SR&nbsp;17. After a short concurrency, the route drops SR&nbsp;17 and continues west with SR&nbsp;133. Eventually, it turns off of the concurrency and heads north, crossing the Tennessee state line directly beside the [[Natchez Trace Parkway]]. The route becomes [[Tennessee State Route 227|Tennessee SR&nbsp;227]], which loops around and acts as the northern terminus to SR&nbsp;101.

This route is an important connector between multiple cities in Northwestern Alabama with SR&nbsp;20, SR&nbsp;24, and [[Alabama State Route 13|SR&nbsp;13]].

==History==
Most of the length of SR&nbsp;157 was constructed between 1957 and 1962.<ref name=ASHD_1957>{{cite map |title = Official 1957 Alabama Highway Map |date = 1957 |url = http://alabamamaps.ua.edu/historicalmaps/stateroads/index2.html |publisher = [[Alabama State Highway Department]] |access-date = February 12, 2021 }}</ref><ref name=ASHD_1963>{{cite map |title = Official 1963 Alabama Highway Map |date = 1963 |url = http://alabamamaps.ua.edu/historicalmaps/stateroads/index2.html |publisher = [[Alabama State Highway Department]] |access-date = February 12, 2021 }}</ref>

In 1969, the route was rerouted in Cullman County onto a new road, from an older two-lane road. This older routing is now discontinuous and impossible to traverse at once without using the new route, compiled of modern-day County Road 1188, County Road 1181, County Road 1174; and (on the east side of Interstate 65) 'Old Highway 157' and St. Joseph Street NW. After that point, SR&nbsp;157 was routed onto the modern-day routing, also a two-lane road. This new routing featured an interchange with Interstate 65 and continued straight to U.S. Route 31. Over the last several years, this routing has been widened and extended to be a continuous four-lane divided highway from U.S. Route 31 to the county line; and now extends all the way to U.S. Route 278 on the east side of the city.<ref>[https://ngmdb.usgs.gov/img4/ht_icons/overlay/AL/AL_Cullman_303623_1957_24000_geo.jpg Map] usgs.gov</ref><ref>[https://ngmdb.usgs.gov/img4/ht_icons/overlay/AL/AL_Cullman_303625_1957_24000_geo.jpg Map] usgs.gov</ref><ref name="auto">{{Cite web |title=Map NGMDB |url=https://ngmdb.usgs.gov/img4/ht_icons/overlay/AL/AL_West%20Point_305363_1958_24000_geo.jpg |website=ngmdb.usgs.gov}} </ref>

The stretch between SR 36 at Oakville and SR 33 at Moulton was constructed later, between 1967 and 1968.

<ref name=ASHD_1967>{{cite map |title = Official 1967 Alabama Highway Map |date = 1967 |url = http://alabamamaps.ua.edu/historicalmaps/stateroads/index2.html |publisher = [[Alabama State Highway Department]] |access-date = February 12, 2021 }}</ref><ref name=ASHD_1969>{{cite map |title = Official 1969 Alabama Highway Map |date = 1969 |url = http://alabamamaps.ua.edu/historicalmaps/stateroads/index2.html |publisher = [[Alabama State Highway Department]] |access-date = February 12, 2021 }}</ref> SR&nbsp;157 has been four-laned along almost its entire length. This project, which began in the 1980s, was finally completed in the summer of 2007.{{citation needed|date=December 2013}}

